 identify the cause or appropriate response."},{"id":"B","text":"A need for immediate respiratory reassessment and escalation","explanation":"Routine inhaled therapy does not replace assessment of deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"A confirmed improvement in bronchodilation","explanation":"Worsening breathing contradicts this interpretation."},{"id":"D","text":"A confirmed isolated medication taste intolerance","explanation":"Taste does not account for worsening oxygenation and work of breathing."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Worsening respiratory symptoms require reassessment beyond routine inhaler use.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-ipratropium--7-day--respiratory_worsening","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-ipratropium::respiratory_worsening"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-ipratropium--advanced--respiratory_worsening","answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-adenosine","title":"ADENOSINE","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-adenosine","itemType":"medication","title":"ADENOSINE","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of ADENOSINE?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Briefly blocks AV-nodal conduction","explanation":"Its very short action requires rapid delivery when used in the indicated rhythm pathway."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduces vagal slowing of the heart","explanation":"This describes Antimuscarinic. Its very short action requires rapid delivery when used in the indicated rhythm pathway."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits Na/K ATPase and increases vagal tone","explanation":"This describes Cardiac glycoside. Its very short action requires rapid delivery when used in the indicated rhythm pathway."},{"id":"D","text":"Blocks fast sodium channels","explanation":"This describes Class Ic antiarrhythmic. Its very short action requires rapid delivery when used in the indicated rhythm pathway."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does ADENOSINE belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Predominantly class III agent","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Ultra-short-acting AV-nodal blocking antiarrhythmic."},{"id":"B","text":"Antimuscarinic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Ultra-short-acting AV-nodal blocking antiarrhythmic."},{"id":"C","text":"Class Ic antiarrhythmic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Ultra-short-acting AV-nodal blocking antiarrhythmic."},{"id":"D","text":"A1-receptor agonist","explanation":"Ultra-short-acting AV-nodal blocking antiarrhythmic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which monitored adult rhythm most clearly matches an indicated acute adenosine trial?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irregular wide-complex tachycardia of uncertain mechanism","explanation":"Adenosine should not be given empirically in irregular wide-complex tachycardia."},{"id":"B","text":"Hemodynamically stable, regular narrow-complex SVT","explanation":"Adenosine is indicated in the stable regular narrow-complex tachycardia pathway."},{"id":"C","text":"Sinus tachycardia caused by untreated fever and dehydration","explanation":"The underlying cause rather than transient AV block needs treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"Irregular atrial fibrillation without an organized SVT circuit","explanation":"Adenosine is not the indicated therapy to terminate atrial fibrillation."}],"answerKey":"B","point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"stem":"Which immediate effect should the team anticipate and monitor during indicated adenosine use?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Gradual renal glucose excretion over several days","explanation":"This is an SGLT2-inhibitor mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Progressive vitamin-K depletion over several days","explanation":"This is not adenosine’s mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"Brief AV block with transient flushing or chest discomfort","explanation":"The short-lived AV-nodal effect can feel frightening and requires monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"Prolonged corticosteroid immunosuppression","explanation":"Adenosine is not a maintenance glucocorticoid."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Brief AV block and unpleasant sensations can occur with adenosine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--adenosine_transient","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::adenosine_transient"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which history most directly changes the respiratory safety review before ADENOSINE?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An old uncomplicated sprained ankle","explanation":"This is not the respiratory warning being tested."},{"id":"B","text":"Active asthma or bronchospasm","explanation":"This is a respiratory safety concern."},{"id":"C","text":"A remote healed superficial wound","explanation":"This does not identify airway susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"Previously controlled constipation","explanation":"This does not establish bronchospasm risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review bronchospasm/asthma risk when identified for the treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--bronchospasm_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::bronchospasm_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For an adult with stable, regular narrow-complex tachycardia, what is the first adenosine dose in the AHA algorithm? Enter mg.","answer":6,"unit":"mg","tolerance":0,"explanation":"The adult sequence begins with 6 mg rapid IV followed by a rapid flush.","point":"The taught adult AHA sequence starts with 6 mg rapid IV.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--adenosine_first","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::adenosine_first"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which delivery sequence is correct for IV adenosine in an adult with stable regular narrow-complex SVT?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prolonged infusion without a flush or rhythm monitoring","explanation":"This is not the delivery method for indicated adenosine."},{"id":"B","text":"Rapid IV push with the flush delayed for minutes","explanation":"Delaying the flush reduces effective delivery of this short-lived medication."},{"id":"C","text":"Slow IV push, delayed saline flush, ECG monitoring","explanation":"Slow administration may allow substantial clearance before AV-nodal exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Rapid IV push, immediate saline flush, ECG monitoring","explanation":"The rapid push and flush support delivery before rapid drug clearance."}],"point":"Use a rapid adenosine IV push immediately followed by saline flush during indicated rhythm treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--adenosine_immediate_flush","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::adenosine_immediate_flush"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"The ordered adenosine dose is injected slowly through long tubing and has little effect. Which explanation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rapid clearance can prevent an adequate AV-nodal exposure","explanation":"Rapid proximal delivery plus immediate flush addresses the drug’s very short action."},{"id":"B","text":"Slower delivery prolongs the AV-nodal exposure through protein binding","explanation":"This does not fit the short-lived action."},{"id":"C","text":"The saline flush chemically activates an otherwise inactive prodrug","explanation":"The flush primarily delivers the drug promptly; it is not an activation reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"A long infusion is required for the drug to accumulate in the AV node","explanation":"This is opposite to the delivery principle."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Rapid IV delivery and saline flush are required for the indicated adenosine treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-adenosine--comprehensive--adenosine_delivery","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-adenosine::adenosine_delivery"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Adenosine has little effect in a patient receiving aminophylline. Which receptor-level explanation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Methylxanthine antagonism reduces adenosine’s effect","explanation":"Aminophylline/theophylline can antagonize adenosine."},{"id":"B","text":"Methylxanthine inhibition of uptake amplifies adenosine","explanation":"This reverses the direction of the interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Additive muscarinic blockade prevents vagal slowing","explanation":"Adenosine is not atropine and this is not the relevant interaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Additive sodium-channel blockade prolongs QRS duration","explanation":"This is not the stated adenosine–aminophylline mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Methylxanthines can reduce adenosine effect; dipyridamol