itoring and immediate airway/ventilation capability.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-ketamine--7-day--sedation_airway","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-ketamine::sedation_airway"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-ketamine--advanced--sedation_airway","answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient taking KETAMINE — ketamine hydrochloride also receives an opioid or another sedative. Which monitoring pair best addresses the interaction described?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oral lesions and swallowing comfort","explanation":"These do not monitor the principal combined sedative effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel sounds and abdominal girth","explanation":"These do not directly assess the immediate central-depressant hazard."},{"id":"C","text":"Tendon tenderness and joint range","explanation":"These assess a different medication-related problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Consciousness and respiratory effort","explanation":"Combined depressant effects can compromise alertness and ventilation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"CNS depressants can compound sedation and respiratory risk.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-ketamine--7-day--cns_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-ketamine::cns_combination"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-ketamine--advanced--cns_combination","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":8}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-pethidine","title":"PETHIDINE — meperidine","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-pethidine--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-pethidine","itemType":"medication","title":"PETHIDINE — meperidine","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of PETHIDINE — meperidine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits cyclooxygenase and reduces prostaglandins","explanation":"This describes NSAID. Mu-receptor agonism provides analgesia and can depress consciousness and breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"Activates mu receptors","explanation":"Mu-receptor agonism provides analgesia and can depress consciousness and breathing."},{"id":"C","text":"Binds the alpha-2-delta calcium-channel subunit","explanation":"This describes Gabapentinoid. Mu-receptor agonism provides analgesia and can depress consciousness and breathing."},{"id":"D","text":"Enhances GABA-A receptor activity","explanation":"This describes Benzodiazepine. Mu-receptor agonism provides analgesia and can depress consciousness and breathing."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-pethidine--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-pethidine::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does PETHIDINE — meperidine belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pyrazolone analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid analgesic","explanation":"Opioid analgesic."},{"id":"D","text":"NSAID","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-pethidine--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-pethidine::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which prescribing goal most closely fits selected monitored pethidine use?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relief of severe pain when the treating clinician chooses this opioid","explanation":"Pethidine (meperidine) is an opioid analgesic reserved for selected pain situations after safety review."},{"id":"B","text":"Routine first-line treatment of all acute coronary chest pain","explanation":"Pethidine is not a universal or preferred immediate treatment for every ACS presentation."},{"id":"C","text":"Long-term control of neuropathic pain in advanced kidney disease","explanation":"Accumulation of neurotoxic metabolites makes repeated pethidine use problematic in renal impairment."},{"id":"D","text":"Reversal of opioid-related hypoventilation in a sedated patient","explanation":"Pethidine itself can suppress respiration and does not reverse opioid toxicity."}],"answerKey":"A","point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-pethidine--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-pethidine::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"stem":"After PETHIDINE — meperidine, which change requires the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New nausea with preserved alertness","explanation":"This is less immediately threatening than reduced responsiveness with abnormal breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"New constipation and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These matter but do not outrank the described ventilatory threat."},{"id":"C","text":"New reduced responsiveness and shallow breathing","explanation":"This suggests a threat to ventilation from opioid toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"New itching without airway or BP change","explanation":"This requires assessment but is less urgent than ventilation impairment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-pethidine--comprehensive--opioid_respiratio