in but also reduces respiratory drive and can lower BP.","availability":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data include a 10-mg morphine ampoule; verify salt, concentration, route and ordered dose."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Strong opioid analgesic.","id":"section-0-d232416c9d3896c8"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Mu-opioid receptor agonist.","id":"section-1-39c0da32a519a0b5"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Selected severe pain/distress management when prescribed; not automatically indicated for every ACS patient or every case of pulmonary edema.","id":"section-2-911ae6fb7347d30a"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV or IM as appropriate to the product/order. IV morphine is given slowly and titrated under close monitoring, not as an unmonitored rapid bolus. Keep naloxone and airway support available.","id":"section-3-6d05c9946f059ceb"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain, RR, SpO2, consciousness and BP before/after. • Sedation can precede respiratory depression. • Hold/escalate for significant respiratory depression, severe hypotension or reduced consciousness; support airway/ventilation and follow naloxone protocol if toxicity is suspected. • Nausea, vomiting, constipation, pruritus and dizziness can occur. • Opioids + benzodiazepines/other sedatives can cause profound respiratory depression. • Follow controlled-drug procedures.","id":"section-4-a898ee6f25ab9d1a"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Mu-opioid activation relieves severe pain but also reduces respiratory drive and can lower BP.","id":"section-5-c61364b1f676b475"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data include a 10-mg morphine ampoule; verify salt, concentration, route and ordered dose.","id":"section-6-ddb08e53adebd1b7"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-morphine--standa